    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "GPT-5.6 Sol on wolfram2002-ch05-06: resolvable-now, resolvability 0.86";
    dc:description "The finite rule spaces, sampling design, and claimed few-per-million frequency make the statement directly testable by computation. Long transients limit perfect asymptotic labels, but they need not prevent a decisive estimate of the stated rarity.";
    dc:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/concept/dataset-resolvability-0.2.0>;
    dc:type "resolution-route/computation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Proposed test with existing data: Enumerate or uniformly sample the precisely defined two-dimensional Turing-machine rule spaces for two through four states, run standardized blank-tape experiments, and classify trajectories after fixed horizons. Estimate complex-rule frequencies with confidence intervals and inspect longer runs for misclassified transients.";
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-gpt-5.6-sol-medium>;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/assessmentType> "dataset-resolvability/0.2.0";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/evidenceAgainst> "Busy-beaver-like long transients can defeat finite classification.", "Complete four-state enumeration may be computationally demanding.", "Normally and complex require declared frequency and behavior thresholds.";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasConfidence> "0.91"^^xsd:double;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasScore> "0.862"^^xsd:double;
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasVerdict> "resolvable-now".
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/assessment/wolfram2002-ch05-06--gpt--analysis-feasibility> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/LLMClaimAssessment>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Analysis feasibility of wolfram2002-ch05-06 by GPT-5.6 Sol: 0.78";
